"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[21937],{321937:e=>{e.exports='# How to refinements\n\n## When to Use Query Refinements\n\n:::warning\n**CRITICAL: Use refinements for existing queries**\n\nQuery refinements should be used when:\n\n- The user wants to filter, sort, or aggregate **existing query results**\n- A query has already been executed and produced results\n- The user says "filter **these** results", "sort **this** data", "remove **these** rows"\n- You want to preserve the original query logic while adding transformations\n\n**DO NOT modify the SQL query directly** - use the `pageUpdateLakewatchQueryRefinements` tool instead.\n:::\n\n## Understanding Refinements\n\nRefinements are SQL clauses that get appended to the base query to transform results. Each refinement is a separate SQL clause string that transforms the result set.\n\n### Field References in Refinements\n\n:::warning\n**CRITICAL: Consider existing refinements when referencing fields**\n\nWhen creating new refinements, you must be aware that existing refinements may have:\n\n- **Renamed columns** using `AS` aliases\n- **Added new computed columns** that are NOT part of the underlying table schema\n- **Transformed fields** through functions or expressions\n\nBefore creating a new refinement:\n\n1. **Review existing refinements** to understand what fields are available\n2. **Verify field references** against both:\n   - The original underlying table schema\n   - Any columns added or renamed by existing refinements\n3. **Use the correct field names** that exist at the point where your refinement will be applied\n\n**Example scenario:**\n\n- Original table has: `user_id`, `event_type`, `timestamp`\n- Existing refinement adds: `SELECT *, HOUR(timestamp) as event_hour`\n- New refinement can reference: `user_id`, `event_type`, `timestamp`, AND `event_hour`\n- New refinement should NOT reference non-existent fields from the original schema\n\n**Invalid refinement:**\n\n```sql\nWHERE event_hour > 12  -- FAILS if \'event_hour\' hasn\'t been created yet\n```\n\n**Valid refinement:**\n\n```sql\nWHERE event_hour > 12  -- WORKS if a previous refinement added \'event_hour\'\n```\n\n:::\n\n## Pre-Refinement Review Checklist\n\n:::danger\n**MANDATORY: ALWAYS review against this checklist before adding ANY refinement**\n\nBefore calling `pageUpdateLakewatchQueryRefinements`, you MUST verify each refinement against ALL items in this checklist:\n:::\n\n### Checklist\n\n✓ **Valid Pipe Query Syntax**\n\n- [ ] Each refinement uses correct pipe query SQL syntax\n- [ ] Proper clause types: `SELECT`, `WHERE`, `ORDER BY`, `GROUP BY`, `LIMIT`, etc.\n- [ ] Correct SQL operators and functions\n- [ ] Properly quoted strings and escaped special characters\n- [ ] Valid comparison operators (`=`, `!=`, `>`, `<`, `>=`, `<=`, `IN`, `LIKE`, etc.)\n\n✓ **Field Reference Validation**\n\n- [ ] All referenced fields exist in either:\n  - The original underlying table schema, OR\n  - Previously applied refinements that added or renamed columns\n- [ ] Check for field names that may have been aliased (e.g., `timestamp AS event_time`)\n- [ ] Verify nested field references use correct dot notation (e.g., `user.name`, `metadata.ip_address`)\n- [ ] Confirm computed columns from previous refinements are available before referencing them\n\n✓ **Refinement Dependencies**\n\n- [ ] If referencing a computed column, verify it was created by an earlier refinement\n- [ ] Understand the order of refinements and their cumulative effect\n- [ ] Ensure the refinement can execute given the current state of the result set\n\n✓ **Semantic Correctness**\n\n- [ ] The refinement achieves the user\'s intended goal\n- [ ] Logic is correct (e.g., `WHERE status = \'active\'` not `WHERE status != \'active\'` if user wants active records)\n- [ ] Aggregations match the user\'s request\n- [ ] Sort order is correct (ASC vs DESC)\n\n✓ **Valid Spark SQL**\n\n- [ ] Each refinement is valid spark SQL syntax\n- [ ] Referenced function names are valid functions that are defined and exist in spark SQL dialect\n- [ ] Do not use functions that only exist in postgres, mysql or other SQL dialects\n\n### Review Process\n\n**Before every `pageUpdateLakewatchQueryRefinements` call:**\n\n1. **Identify available fields** by reviewing:\n   - The original table schema\n   - Any existing refinements that added/renamed columns\n\n2. **Validate syntax** of each refinement clause\n\n3. **Verify field references** exist in the available field set\n\n4. **Test logic** mentally: will this refinement produce the desired result?\n\n5. **Only proceed** if ALL checklist items pass\n\n**Example Review:**\n\n```typescript\n// User request: "Filter to events after 2pm"\n// Existing refinements: ["SELECT *, HOUR(timestamp) as event_hour"]\n\n// REVIEW:\n// ✓ Syntax: "WHERE event_hour >= 14" - valid WHERE clause\n// ✓ Field: \'event_hour\' exists (added by previous refinement)\n// ✓ Dependencies: Previous refinement created \'event_hour\'\n// ✓ Semantics: >= 14 correctly filters for 2pm and later\n// ✓ APPROVED: Safe to add\n```\n\n## Tool Usage: pageUpdateLakewatchQueryRefinements\n\nWhen the user requests refinements, use the `pageUpdateLakewatchQueryRefinements` tool.\n\n:::warning\n**REQUIRED: Complete the Pre-Refinement Review Checklist (above) before calling this tool**\n\nDo not proceed without verifying all checklist items.\n:::\n\n### Tool Parameters\n\n```typescript\n{\n  thoughts: string;        // Your reasoning for these refinements\n                          // MUST include confirmation that checklist was reviewed\n  refinements: string[];   // Array of SQL clause strings\n                          // MUST pass all checklist validation items\n}\n```\n\n### Single Refinement Example\n\n**User request:** _"Filter these results to only show events from the US"_\n\n**Tool call:**\n\n```json\n{\n  "thoughts": "User wants to filter existing results to only US events. Checklist review: ✓ Valid WHERE syntax, ✓ \'country\' field exists in table schema, ✓ Semantically correct. Adding a WHERE clause refinement.",\n  "refinements": ["WHERE country = \'US\'"]\n}\n```\n\n### Multiple Refinements Example\n\n**User request:** _"Show only successful logins from the US, sorted by time descending"_\n\n**Tool call:**\n\n```json\n{\n  "thoughts": "User wants to filter to US successful logins and sort by time. Checklist review: ✓ All clauses use valid syntax (WHERE, ORDER BY), ✓ Fields \'country\', \'status\', \'time\' all exist in table schema, ✓ Logic correct for filtering and sorting. Adding two WHERE refinements and one ORDER BY refinement.",\n  "refinements": ["WHERE country = \'US\'", "WHERE status = \'Success\'", "ORDER BY time DESC"]\n}\n```\n\n## Types of Refinements\n\n:::info\n**Field Validation Reminder**\n\nBefore creating any refinement, verify that the fields you reference exist in either:\n\n- The original table schema, OR\n- Previous refinements that added or renamed columns\n\nAlways check existing refinements to understand the current field landscape.\n:::\n\n### 1. Filtering (WHERE Clauses)\n\nFilter rows based on column values.\n\n**User request:** _"Filter these results to only show events from the US"_\n\n**Refinement:**\n\n```sql\nWHERE country = \'US\'\n```\n\n_Note: Verify that \'country\' exists in the table schema or was added by a previous refinement_\n\n**User request:** _"Remove rows where status is \'test\'"_\n\n**Refinement:**\n\n```sql\nWHERE status != \'test\'\n```\n\n_Note: Verify that \'status\' exists in the table schema or was added by a previous refinement_\n\n### 2. Sorting (ORDER BY Clauses)\n\nSort results by one or more columns.\n\n**User request:** _"Sort by the timestamp column descending"_\n\n**Refinement:**\n\n```sql\nORDER BY time DESC\n```\n\n### 3. Grouping and Aggregation (GROUP BY Clauses)\n\nGroup results and apply aggregate functions.\n\n**User request:** _"Group these results by user and count events"_\n\n**Refinements:**\n\n```json\n["GROUP BY user.name", "SELECT user.name, COUNT(*) as event_count"]\n```\n\n_Note: This adds \'event_count\' as a new field that subsequent refinements can reference_\n\n### Example: Building Refinements Sequentially\n\n**Scenario:** User progressively refines query results\n\n**Step 1:** Original query returns columns: `user_id`, `event_type`, `timestamp`\n\n**Step 2:** User says _"Add a column for the hour of day"_\n\n**Refinement 1:**\n\n```sql\nSELECT *, HOUR(timestamp) as event_hour\n```\n\n**Step 3:** User says _"Now filter to only show events after 2pm"_\n\n**Refinement 2:** (builds on Refinement 1)\n\n```sql\nWHERE event_hour >= 14\n```\n\n_✓ Valid: References \'event_hour\' which was added by Refinement 1_\n\n**Step 4:** User says _"Count events by hour"_\n\n**Refinement 3:** (builds on Refinement 1)\n\n```json\n["SELECT event_hour, COUNT(*) as hourly_count", "GROUP BY event_hour"]\n```\n\n_✓ Valid: References \'event_hour\' from Refinement 1 and adds \'hourly_count\'_\n\n**Invalid Example:**\n\n```sql\nWHERE hourly_count > 10\n```\n\n_✗ Invalid: Cannot reference \'hourly_count\' unless Refinement 3 was applied first_\n\n### 4. Flattening Structured Columns (SELECT with Nested Field Access)\n\nFlatten or extract fields from structured columns (STRUCT, VARIANT, MAP, or JSON objects).\n\n:::warning\n**CRITICAL: Validate nested field references against schema**\n\nWhen flattening structured columns:\n\n1. **Inspect the column\'s schema** to determine available keys/fields\n2. **Check both sources:**\n   - Original table schema (for the base structured column)\n   - Current result set schema (may include computed structured columns from previous refinements)\n3. **Only reference fields that actually exist** in the structure\n4. **Use correct syntax** for the column type:\n   - STRUCT: `column_name.field_name`\n   - MAP: `column_name[\'key_name\']`\n   - JSON string: `column_name:field_name` or `GET_JSON_OBJECT(column_name, \'$.field_name\')`\n   - VARIANT: `column_name.field_name` or `column_name[\'key_name\']`\n     :::\n\n**User request:** _"Help me flatten http_request object"_\n\n**Scenario:** Table has an `http_request` column of type STRUCT with fields: `method`, `url`, `status_code`, `user_agent`\n\n**Step 1: Inspect the schema** (from either the original table schema or current result set)\n\n```\nhttp_request: STRUCT<\n  method: STRING,\n  url: STRING,\n  status_code: INT,\n  user_agent: STRING\n>\n```\n\n**Step 2: Create refinement using ONLY the available fields**\n\n**Refinement:**\n\n```sql\nSELECT *, http_request.method, http_request.url, http_request.status_code, http_request.user_agent\n```\n\n**Tool call:**\n\n```json\n{\n  "thoughts": "User wants to flatten the http_request STRUCT column. Checklist review: ✓ Inspected schema and confirmed http_request has fields: method, url, status_code, user_agent. ✓ Valid SELECT syntax with nested field access. ✓ All referenced fields exist in the STRUCT. ✓ Semantically correct - extracts nested fields as top-level columns.",\n  "refinements": ["SELECT *, http_request.method, http_request.url, http_request.status_code, http_request.user_agent"]\n}\n```\n\n**Alternative: Selective flattening with aliases**\n\n**User request:** _"Extract just the HTTP method and status code from http_request"_\n\n**Refinement:**\n\n```sql\nSELECT *, http_request.method AS http_method, http_request.status_code AS status\n```\n\n**Tool call:**\n\n```json\n{\n  "thoughts": "User wants to extract specific fields from http_request STRUCT. Checklist review: ✓ Verified \'method\' and \'status_code\' fields exist in http_request STRUCT schema. ✓ Valid SELECT with field aliases. ✓ Semantically correct. Adding these as new columns: http_method, status.",\n  "refinements": ["SELECT *, http_request.method AS http_method, http_request.status_code AS status"]\n}\n```\n\n**Invalid Example: Referencing non-existent fields**\n\n```sql\nSELECT *, http_request.method, http_request.headers, http_request.body\n```\n\n_✗ Invalid: \'headers\' and \'body\' do not exist in the http_request STRUCT schema. Only reference fields that are actually present: method, url, status_code, user_agent_\n\n**Example with MAP type**\n\n**Scenario:** Table has `metadata` column of type `MAP<STRING, STRING>`\n\n**User request:** _"Extract the ip_address from metadata"_\n\n**Refinement:**\n\n```sql\nSELECT *, metadata[\'ip_address\'] AS ip_address\n```\n\n**Example with JSON string**\n\n**Scenario:** Table has `json_payload` column containing JSON strings\n\n**User request:** _"Extract the user id from the JSON payload"_\n\n**Refinement (Databricks syntax):**\n\n```sql\nSELECT *, json_payload:user_id AS user_id\n```\n\n**Or using function syntax:**\n\n```sql\nSELECT *, GET_JSON_OBJECT(json_payload, \'$.user_id\') AS user_id\n```\n\n**Multi-step flattening example**\n\n**Scenario:** After flattening http_request, user wants to filter by method\n\n**Step 1: Flatten the structure**\n\n```sql\nSELECT *, http_request.method AS http_method, http_request.status_code AS status\n```\n\n**Step 2: Filter using the newly created column**\n\n```sql\nWHERE http_method = \'POST\'\n```\n\n**Tool call:**\n\n```json\n{\n  "thoughts": "User first wants http_request flattened, then filtered. Checklist review: ✓ First refinement extracts valid fields \'method\' and \'status_code\' from http_request STRUCT, creating \'http_method\' and \'status\' columns. ✓ Second refinement correctly references \'http_method\' which will be created by the first refinement. ✓ Valid WHERE syntax. ✓ Semantically correct.",\n  "refinements": [\n    "SELECT *, http_request.method AS http_method, http_request.status_code AS status",\n    "WHERE http_method = \'POST\'"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n_✓ Valid: The second refinement references \'http_method\' which is created by the first refinement_\n\n## Refinements vs. Query Modification\n\n### When to Use Refinements\n\n✓ Use refinements when:\n\n- User says "filter **these** results", "sort **this** data"\n- A query has already been executed\n- User wants to explore results interactively\n- Changes are temporary and exploratory\n- User wants to preserve the original query\n\n### When to Modify the Query\n\n✗ Modify the query SQL when:\n\n- User wants to change the data source (FROM clause)\n- User needs different columns in the output\n- User wants to change the base aggregation logic\n- User requests optimization for performance\n- User explicitly asks to "change the query" or "rewrite the SQL"\n'}}]);
